WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.000
When someone needs help, a phone call should be enough. Pharos turns that call into a clear next step for responders.

2
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Here’s a short excerpt from a real call.

original-call
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:23.000
[Original real phone-call excerpt with fictional scenario details]

3
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:35.000
That call is now complete. The team can read the generated transcript, check consent, and open the linked request. Nothing has to be copied by hand.

4
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Overview brings the essentials together: where the caller is, what they need, and why the request is ranked this way. These are reported facts for people to review.

5
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:58.000
For this fictional training report, the team needs to check a concern. The quoted words stay beside the request. Reported danger keeps its urgency while normal response actions wait for human review.

6
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:12.000
The reviewer records a reason and accepts the report. Now an eligible responder can take ownership. That decision stays tied to the evidence, so everyone can see what was checked.

7
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Here, the desk reports that the responder is on the way and gives a ten-minute estimate. A claim alone never promises travel, and an estimate is not a guarantee.

8
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:35.000
History keeps the report and human decisions together. When new information arrives, the team can look back, reassess the situation, and update the next step.

9
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Coverage shows which approved teams serve each area, what help they can offer, and whether they have room for another request.

10
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:58.000
The training lab lets teams practice with a prepared fictional report. Process it once, then review the new case. That’s Pharos: a clearer handoff from a caller to the people who can help.

